[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=Openote
GenericName=Notebook
Comment=Open-source, natively cross-platform alternative to OneNote
# Absolute, not a bare name: the package installs the app under /opt and only
# symlinks it onto PATH. A bare `Exec=openote` works from a terminal but some
# launchers resolve the entry without the user's PATH, and the failure is a
# menu icon that does nothing when clicked.
#
# `%f` — ONE file, as a path. It is what makes double-clicking a notebook in
# the file manager open it. A DIRECTORY arrives through `%f` as an ordinary
# path too, which is what makes the `inode/directory` subclass in openote.xml
# worth declaring at all: `main()` notices the path is a `.onotebook` and routes
# it to the join path rather than to the container opener
# (app/lib/core/open_target.dart). This used to be deliberately absent, with
# the comment that "`main()` in app/lib/main.dart takes no arguments and always
# restores the last session", so claiming the association would have launched
# Openote showing a DIFFERENT notebook. That is fixed: `main(List<String> args)`
# reads the path (app/lib/core/startup_args.dart) and opens it, and a second
# double-click while Openote is running is handed to the running copy rather
# than starting a second one (app/lib/core/single_instance.dart).
#
# `%f` and not `%F` or `%U`. `%F` offers a list, and Openote has one workspace
# window, so the second and third files would be silently dropped — better not
# to claim them. `%U` would hand over a URL for a file the desktop had to mount
# first, which is a promise this app cannot keep. (The argument parser still
# accepts a `file://` URL, because some launchers send one regardless of what
# the entry asked for.)
Exec=/opt/openote/openote %f
Icon=openote
Categories=Office;Utility;
Terminal=false
# The types declared by openote.xml, which the package installs into
# /usr/share/mime/packages. Declaring them here is the half that makes this app
# the HANDLER; the .xml on its own only makes them described and iconed. Both
# are refreshed by the postinst's `update-desktop-database` and
# `update-mime-database`, so the association is live without a logout — and the
# same refresh is what RETIRES `application/x-onote`, which this line used to
# claim and which openote.xml no longer declares.
#
# The notebook folder first, the pointer file second. The order is the
# preference a desktop reads when both could apply, and the folder is the
# notebook; the pointer file inside it is the fallback for shells that will not
# open a directory as a document.
MimeType=application/x-onote-notebook;application/x-onote-link;
# Must match what the running window actually reports, or the launcher shows a
# second, generic icon beside the real one instead of highlighting it.
# app/linux/runner/my_application.cc:138 calls `g_set_prgname(APPLICATION_ID)`,
# and APPLICATION_ID is "org.openote.openote" (app/linux/CMakeLists.txt:10) —
# NOT "openote". The file is named for the same id for the same reason: modern
# desktops, Wayland especially, match a window to its entry by that basename.
StartupWMClass=org.openote.openote
Keywords=notes;notebook;onenote;handwriting;ink;
